1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,519
Let's take stock.

2
00:00:01,519 --> 00:00:03,551
Least squares is a projection,

3
00:00:03,551 --> 00:00:06,396
and among linear unbiased estimates the best.

4
00:00:06,396 --> 00:00:08,427
Ridge shrinks the weak directions;

5
00:00:08,427 --> 00:00:10,866
the lasso's soft thresholds choose words.

6
00:00:10,866 --> 00:00:13,304
Logistic regression is a convex bowl,

7
00:00:13,304 --> 00:00:17,368
and Newton's method finds its bottom in a few steps.

8
00:00:17,368 --> 00:00:20,212
Naive Bayes learns fast but stays wrong,

9
00:00:20,212 --> 00:00:22,244
and overconfident on these pages.

10
00:00:22,244 --> 00:00:25,089
In the large-sample limit, a single neighbour

11
00:00:25,089 --> 00:00:29,152
errs at most twice as often as the best rule,

12
00:00:29,152 --> 00:00:31,591
but neighbours drown in many dimensions.

13
00:00:31,591 --> 00:00:34,435
Kernels lift without lifting, and the support

14
00:00:34,435 --> 00:00:36,873
vector machine's dual needs only them.

15
00:00:36,873 --> 00:00:41,750
Averaging trees cuts variance down to a floor set by their correlation;

16
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:44,188
boosting walks down the exponential loss,

17
00:00:44,188 --> 00:00:46,220
and XGBoost takes Newton steps.

18
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:52,596
And an honest score needs the right curve and untouched test pages.

19
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:54,880
All of it comes back.

20
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,335
Cross-entropy, gradients and curvature are how

21
00:00:57,335 --> 00:01:01,019
every network in Parts 9 and 11 is trained,

22
00:01:01,019 --> 00:01:04,702
and the softmax is logistic regression with many classes.

23
00:01:04,702 --> 00:01:07,157
Part 7 builds the statistical models:

24
00:01:07,157 --> 00:01:08,794
principal components, mixtures, Bayes.

25
00:01:08,794 --> 00:01:12,478
Part 8 asks why a model generalizes at all,

26
00:01:12,478 --> 00:01:14,933
and meets the neural tangent kernel,

27
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:18,207
a network that behaves like a kernel machine.

28
00:01:18,207 --> 00:01:21,481
Part 13 reads attention as a kernel smoother,

29
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:24,756
and Part 31 asks the same question of

30
00:01:24,756 --> 00:01:28,030
language models that we asked of naive Bayes:

31
00:01:28,030 --> 00:01:31,167
are its probabilities honest?

32
00:01:31,433 --> 00:01:36,080
Here are eight problems to try, from Gauss-Markov to XGBoost's gain.

33
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:38,615
Pause here, and work through them;

34
00:01:38,615 --> 00:01:43,072
full solutions are in this part's notes.

35
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:49,915
Next time:

36
00:01:49,915 --> 00:01:51,138
statistical machine learning.

37
00:01:51,138 --> 00:01:56,029
Every method in this part was told who wrote each training page.

38
00:01:56,029 --> 00:02:01,328
Part 7 takes the labels away and asks what structure is there anyway:

39
00:02:01,328 --> 00:02:03,774
the directions the pages spread along,

40
00:02:03,774 --> 00:02:09,757
the clusters they fall into, and the hidden causes behind them.

41
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:13,713
For more, The Elements of Statistical Learning by Hastie,

42
00:02:13,713 --> 00:02:16,575
Tibshirani and Friedman covers every method here,

43
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:19,845
by three of the people who built them;

44
00:02:19,845 --> 00:02:21,889
Bishop's Pattern Recognition and Machine

45
00:02:21,889 --> 00:02:23,934
Learning gives the probabilistic view;

46
00:02:23,934 --> 00:02:27,613
and Schölkopf and Smola's Learning with Kernels the kernels.

47
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:31,293
Thanks for watching, and see you in Part 7.
